+++ linux-7.2.8/drivers/media/platform/amlogic/amvdec/CHANGES.md	2026-10-02 08:55:53.000000000 +0200
@@ -0,0 +1,222 @@
+# meson-amvdec (openucode) — rebuilt on the public amlogic-vdec/rfc driver
+
+This tree is the stateless `meson-amvdec` V4L2 driver of the public series,
+with the openucode firmware as its only firmware.  It replaces the previous
+openucode driver (old base "codex 2d01256" plus the open-firmware paths).
+
+## Base
+
+* Public driver (gus bourg's stateless rework of Maxime Jourdan's meson vdec
+  driver): `gusbourg/linux`, branch `amlogic-vdec/rfc`, commit
+  **0b9101e** ("media: amlogic: amvdec: add Christian Hewitt to the
+  copyright notices"), `drivers/media/platform/amlogic/amvdec/`.
+  The series assumes 7.3-rc4 plus its uapi patch 01382aa
+  (`V4L2_PIX_FMT_AM21C`).
+* Christian Hewitt's patches applied on top (authorship: Christian Hewitt
+  <christianshewitt@gmail.com>):
+  * `08487bcded5f` fixup! media: amlogic: amvdec: tidy the platform format
+    tables (H.264 entries spelled out per SoC, entries sorted by pixel
+    format, table comments dropped);
+  * `b466b4e829b2` media: amlogic: amvdec: accept HEVC coded sizes up to
+    4096x2304 (applied to G12A/G12B/SM1; on GX the HEVC limit is set to
+    1920x1088 below, which supersedes it there).
+* Christian Hewitt's patches already in the base: `257abf0df800` (accept the
+  initial HEVC long-term RPS value, = b068de2) and `f64ea5ab8325` (VP9 frame
+  control default, = 158425b).
+* Not applied: `c4e9fd682d09` (reset the GX HEVC core on restream) — it
+  belongs to the vendor HEVC restream path, which no longer exists.
+
+## What the driver offers
+
+| SoC                | MPEG-2 | H.264 | HEVC | VP9 |
+|--------------------|--------|-------|------|-----|
+| GXBB/GXL/GXLX/GXM  | 1920x1088, NV12M | 1920x1088, NV12M, 16..32 buffers | 8-bit (Main), 1920x1088, NV12M | — |
+| G12A/G12B, SM1     | 1920x1088, NV12M | 3840x2160, NV12M, 16..32 buffers | Main + Main 10, 4096x2304, NV12M + AM21C | profile 0 + 2, 3840x2160, NV12M + AM21C |
+
+Firmware (`firmware_path` and `MODULE_FIRMWARE`), nothing else:
+`meson/vdec/ompeg2_sl.bin`, `oh264_sl.bin`, `ohevc_sl.bin`, `ovp9_sl.bin`.
+Both loaders (`amvdec_1_load_firmware()`, `amvdec_hevc_load_firmware()`)
+refuse any other name (`meson_amvdec_fw_name_ok()`, amvdec_platform.c).
+
+**HEVC on GX** is offered by default (owner's decision for this testing
+driver): 8-bit only, 1920x1088, NV12M.  GXBB inter pictures were wrong until
+ohevc-sl v0.2.2 (2026-10-01: the GX DDR->LMEM DMA is reliable only in
+4-halfword pieces); verified on the Odroid-C2 through this driver, 125/140
+vectors identical (the rest are long-term references, FFmpeg's own SAODBLK
+errors and Main 10, which GX refuses).  The GXL/GXLX/GXM profile has not run
+on silicon.  VP9 has no hardware path on GX.
+
+## Ported (open-firmware behaviour, today's defaults)
+
+* **MPEG-2** (`codec_mpeg12.c`): ompeg2-sl record (osl_build_record /
+  osl_write_record, unchanged), one command per picture and per field (the
+  record carries the pairing), `sequence_end_code` after the picture, the
+  grey NV12 stand-in for references nothing decoded (esparser leaves the
+  reference unresolved, the codec substitutes), 0x80/0x81 verdicts with
+  concealment flagged as an error buffer, 16 KiB workspace.
+* **H.264** (`codec_h264.c`): oh264-sl record and slice table, driver-owned
+  FIFO, per-slice header parse, shared reference-list builder, field pairs,
+  `h264_pipeline` (default on) with 2 pipeline slots, 32 CAPTURE slots
+  (`max_buffers = 32` in the tables; colocated MV store for 32 slots, always
+  sized for two MVs per MB), record/workspace layout, scaling matrix from
+  the control, errors fail the picture alone.  Function bodies of the whole
+  open path are identical to the tested driver (see "Checks").
+* **HEVC** (`codec_hevc.c`, `codec_hevc_open_rec.h` unchanged, ABI record v3
+  / ohevc-sl v0.2.1): per-picture core reset + image reload, `hevc_open_fbc8`
+  default 1 (G12A/G12B/SM1, NV12M), per-picture linear references when a
+  current-RPS reference is missing in 8-bit FBC mode, grey / nearest-POC
+  stand-ins only for current-RPS entries, Main 10 FBC + double write, AM21C,
+  GX profiles (no FBC/AM21C/Main 10/fbc8 there, 64 KiB aligned planes on
+  GXBB), SPS `try_ctrl` refusals: not 8/10-bit, unequal depths, non-4:2:0,
+  one CTB column with several rows; the record builder refuses more than
+  8192 lines (and tile/slice layouts it cannot express).
+* **VP9** (`codec_vp9.c`, `codec_vp9_open_rec.h` unchanged): ovp9-sl,
+  profile 0 + 2, `vp9_open_pipeline` default on, `vp9_open_fbc8` default on,
+  `vp9_open_reload` default on, host backward adaptation
+  (`adapt_coef_probs()`, identical to the tested code), AMLV envelope in a
+  driver-owned stream buffer.
+* **FBC body lifetime** (`codec_hevc_common.c`): the tested implementation —
+  CAPTURE `mem_ops` wrap vb2-dma-contig so an AM21C body belongs to the
+  CAPTURE memory and every exported DMA-BUF; the codec holds references on
+  the memory it programmed; AM21C with imported DMA-BUFs is refused at
+  `queue_setup`.  The public base's parked chunk pool is gone.
+* **Core**: `WQ_HIGHPRI | WQ_UNBOUND` feed/retire workqueue
+  (`amvdec_queue_esparser()`), `vdec1_clk_hz`, `meson_amvdec_request_detach()`
+  / `meson_amvdec_request_complete()` for the VP9 pipeline,
+  `meson_amvdec_canvas_nv12_paddr_spec()` for the MPEG-2 grey surface,
+  `meson_amvdec_hevc_fw_reset_reload()` / `meson_amvdec_hevc_fw_start()`,
+  the older-kernel compat block in `amvdec.h`, `BUNDLE_H264=1`.
+
+## Removed
+
+* All vendor-firmware code: the vendor HEVC and VP9 decoders (RPM parsing,
+  workspace, MPRED/SAO/ANC programming state machines, stall recovery,
+  restream at 1 GiB, request input-end tracking), the vendor multi-instance
+  H.264 state machine (slice-head/config/picture-done ISR, extended firmware
+  swap segments, workspace/SEI/AUX/LMEM buffers, per-picture VLD context,
+  in-band SPS/PPS synthesis and its cache, frame/field preflights,
+  H264_BUFFER_INFO list writes), the vendor MPEG-2 path (CC buffer, parked
+  second field resume, MREG protocol), HEVC header synthesis
+  (`codec_hevc_synth.h`) and SPS RPS resolution (`codec_hevc_rps.h`), H.264
+  SPS/PPS writers in `codec_h264_synth.h`, the VP9 AMLV header prepend in
+  the parser path, the parser-fed request tail buffer.
+* The switches `mpeg12_open_fw`, `h264_open_fw`, `hevc_open_fw`,
+  `vp9_open_fw` and the latches behind them; `stateless` (the public driver
+  is stateless only); every message and doc line that told users to set
+  `*_open_fw=0`.
+* Vendor firmware names from the tables and `MODULE_FIRMWARE`.
+
+## Adaptations to the public restructuring
+
+1. **Request feeding (`esparser.c`)**: the public `esparser_queue()` flow is
+   kept (defer before touching controls, `meson_amvdec_request_begin()`,
+   `v4l2_ctrl_request_setup()`, per-codec control snapshot, timestamp
+   reference resolution, `esparser_src_error()`); its vendor tail (parser
+   DMA write, HEVC header synthesis and cache, VP9 header prepend, request
+   tail buffer, input endpoint, restream) is replaced by the three codec
+   feeds (`..._hevc_open_feed()`, `..._vp9_open_feed()`,
+   `..._mpeg12_sl_feed()`), each taking the job credit as before.  HEVC no
+   longer checks the extended SPS RPS tables per request (ignored, as in the
+   tested path).  H.264 keeps the public path (scan copy, preflight, set
+   params, validation, slice refs); the in-band header step became
+   `meson_amvdec_codec_h264_set_scaling()` (the record carries the matrix).
+   The VP9 pipeline gate (`..._vp9_open_busy()`) and the VP9 retirement in
+   the worker are added where the tested driver had them.
+2. **Timestamps (`amvdec_helpers.c`)**: `meson_amvdec_add_ts()` no longer
+   snapshots VP9/HEVC controls (only the vendor completion read them);
+   `set_input_end`, `flush_ts`, `dst_buf_prepare_hevc`,
+   `dst_buf_prepare_stateless` removed; `struct amvdec_timestamp`,
+   `request_job` and the session lose the snapshot, tail-buffer and
+   header-cache fields.
+3. **H.264**: the public codec file is the base; its stop routine is named
+   `codec_h264_multi_stop_processor()` (was `..._vendor_stop()`, identical
+   body); the public driver has no `resume` op — CAPTURE canvases are mapped
+   on first use in `codec_h264_multi_pick_capture()` (`OH264_MAX_SLOTS`), as
+   the tested stateless path already did; `codec_h264_start()` is the open
+   start, which still uses the public `codec_h264_multi_host_config()`
+   (geometry from the negotiated format, colocated store for every slot).
+4. **Platform tables**: the public per-SoC tables (with the tidy fixup) are
+   kept; the H.264 32-buffer limit moved from a runtime function into the
+   tables; GX HEVC is 8-bit Main only at 1920x1088; GX VP9 entries dropped.
+5. **Core (`amvdec.c`)**: public stateless-only core kept (format handling,
+   buffer counts, request/claim locking, job watch, control defaults and
+   menus).  Ported onto it: CAPTURE `mem_ops`, AM21C DMA-BUF refusal, the
+   workqueue, VP9 pipeline discard on a failed stop.  HEVC `try_ctrl` is the
+   open-firmware rule set; the extended RPS controls are registered only
+   when the kernel defines them (the public LT-RPS relaxation stays).
+6. **Workqueue lifetime**: freed through a devm action instead of in
+   `remove()`, so a probe failure after the allocation no longer leaks it.
+7. **FBC**: the tested `codec_hevc_common.c/.h` replace the public ones
+   whole; the public change to them (non-MMU FBC references allocated with
+   `__GFP_NOWARN` and a rate-limited error) is applied on top.  Vendor-only
+   helpers (`setup_decode_head`, `verify_fbc_guards`, `fill_mmu_map`) are
+   removed, `setup_buffers`/`free_mmu_headers` made static.
+8. **Registers**: the public headers dropped unused registers; re-added the
+   ones the open paths use (`HEVC_DBLK_CFG3`, `AV_SCRATCH_K`, and for the
+   development dump `HEVC_SHIFTED_DATA`, `HEVC_PARSER_INT_STATUS`).
+9. **AM21C fourcc**: the public name `V4L2_PIX_FMT_AM21C`; defined from the
+   LibreELEC kernel's `V4L2_PIX_FMT_MESON_AM21C` (same fourcc) or locally
+   when the header has neither.
+10. **`amvdec_hevc.c`**: `meson_amvdec_hevc_quiesce_reset()` export and the
+    restream routine are gone (vendor only); the open per-picture reset is
+    the tested `fw_reset_reload()` (with the GX sub-engine reset).
+11. **Compat**: `kzalloc_obj()` shim added for kernels without it.
+12. **Parser/VIFIFO**: still powered and allocated (16 MiB) for the non
+    direct-input formats exactly as before, although no path feeds it.
+
+## Module parameters
+
+Default build:
+
+| parameter | default | |
+|---|---|---|
+| `h264_pipeline` | Y | H.264 frame-picture pipelining |
+| `hevc_open_fbc8` | 1 | HEVC 8-bit NV12M through FBC references (G12/SM1) |
+| `hevc_open_timeout_ms` | 1000 | |
+| `vp9_open_pipeline` | Y | |
+| `vp9_open_fbc8` | Y | |
+| `vp9_open_reload` | Y | |
+| `vp9_open_timeout_ms` | 1000 | |
+| `vdec1_clk_hz` | 666666666 | 800000000 (G12/SM1) or 500000000 accepted |
+| `dma_guards` | N | public |
+| `job_watch_ms` | 1000 | public |
+
+The `_open_` names are kept so existing scripts keep working.
+
+Development build only (`make ... OUCODE_DEV=1`, defines
+`AMVDEC_OUCODE_DEV`): the `amrisc-test` debugfs harness (`amrisc_test.c`,
+session-less VDEC_1 / HEVC-core power brackets), `mpeg12_bench`,
+`h264_bench`, `h264_prof`, `hevc_bench`, `hevc_prof`, `hevc_prof_pc`,
+`hevc_open_debug` (incl. the forced linear-reference test bits 16/17),
+`hevc_open_early_reset` (experiment, measured no gain), `vp9_bench`,
+`vp9_bench_startup_us`, `hevc_dbg`.  In the default build these are
+compile-time zero: the code paths fold away and the parameters do not
+exist.
+
+## Build
+
+    export PATH=/home/dev/LibreELEC.tv/build.LibreELEC-VIM3.aarch64-13.0-devel/toolchain/bin:$PATH
+    make -C <VIM3 kernel> M=$PWD ARCH=arm64 CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-libreelec-linux-gnu- W=1 modules \
+         [OUCODE_DEV=1] [BUNDLE_H264=1]
+
+Default, `OUCODE_DEV=1`, `BUNDLE_H264=1` and both: zero warnings with W=1
+against the VIM3 7.3-rc2 kernel.  Native builds on older kernels (Armbian
+6.18/7.1 on GXBB) use the same compat level as the previous driver plus the
+`kzalloc_obj()` shim; not compiled here (no such tree on the host).
+
+## Checks
+
+* `tools/ohevc_rec_*`, `tools/ovp9_rec_*` (copied from the tested driver,
+  build with `-I..`): the record headers are byte-identical to the tested
+  ones.  HEVC, 147 JCT-VC-HEVC_V1 vectors + 21 synthetic cases, modes nv12,
+  fbc+nv12, fbc+nv12 with `HEVC_LINREF=all` and `=alt`, fbc: 9271 pictures,
+  51032 slice segments, 12,315,276 record words — 0 word differences on every
+  built picture; the only "differing" pictures (117, 5 streams) are the
+  designed refusals (WPP_D one CTB column, PICSIZE_A/B/D taller than the
+  firmware limit, TSUNEQBD unequal depths), output identical to the tested
+  driver's header.  VP9, 305 fluster vectors + 13 profile-2 files (streams-p2) + 10 synthetic
+  streams (328), 5414 frames per mode, modes nv12 / fbc / fbc+nv12:
+  0 differences, 3 unsupported frames refused by both.
+* MPEG-2 and H.264 have no userspace record tool; their record builders and
+  the whole open path compare function-for-function identical (comments
+  aside) to the tested driver.
